er acceptance speech having  to thank the police who she  said she certainly needed over the past  few weeks  yeah i think you know you want  by-election campaigns to be hard-fought  you want the debate to be rigorous you  want it to be passionate  um and as part of that there will  sometimes be um  frayed uh tempers and irritations  but i do think there were there were  some people that stood in this  election who were trying to um  whose priority wasn't even really to win  it but it was to try and divide into  damage um the community and i think  what was clear out of the result while  it was very close between uh the labour  party and the conservative party  what was clear is that the people of  batli and spain had no time  for that division the far-right parties  got less than  0.5 percent of the vote combined they  all lost their deposits  so that's one of the most heartening  things i'll take out of this that yes  people turned up and they tried  to put petrol on the flames of those  community divisions  but ultimately the people in batley and  spen  um answered that with a resounding no  and voted for  candidates that did want to bring  communities together george galloway  though ended up taking votes from both  labour and  the tories coming in third place and  joe sees her majority at 323  what do you think the challenges are now  for her ahead both in healing the  divides in the community  and building that majority  i think the easy thing to do um in  communities  specifically when the community's got um  large differences within them whether  that's on the basis of racial religion  it's very easy to come in and stoke  those differences what is hard and what  politics at its best is about  is understanding those divisions and  those differences but trying to forge  a collective vision and a collective  project for those  for those communities so it's it's the  easiest thing in politics to do which is  to  fan the flames of hate and galloway and  some of the others on the far right  are very practiced and very adept at  doing that however as i said  it didn't work ultimately um the two  main parties got the vast majority of  the boats the vote between them  and i think what kim will be focusing on  now is she's been talking about  are those local issues that actually do  bring people together and whether that  is around  education or the local environment or  potholes  that saying that kim um sorry that joe  uh talked about that we have more in  common than the things that divide us  i think it absolutely comes through when  you start talking about the issues  and stop trying to to fan the flames of  of hatred and division  okay uh brendan cox good to hear from  you thank you
 

